## 1. Scope

This reference covers fuse body tubes for high voltage fuses in classes from 1 kV to 36 kV. A fuse body is a pressure vessel during interruption as well as an insulator in service, so the requirements are wider than a dielectric specification.

## 2. Construction

- Resin impregnated glass or fibre wall machined to the required bore, outside diameter and length.
- End features such as threads, holes or grooves machined after cure, which is where most handling damage originates.
- Arc quenching liner inside the bore in expulsion and current limiting designs.
- Concentricity and bore straightness are the tolerances that decide whether the fuse assembles and interrupts consistently.

## 3. Typical specification range

| Parameter | Typical value | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Voltage classes | 1 kV to 36 kV, across the fuse families produced in this range | Confirm the class against the fuse design, not the tube dimensions alone. |
| Wall form | Resin impregnated glass or fibre, machined to bore and length | Wall build and liner material are selected with the fuse designer. |
| End features | Threads, holes, grooves machined to drawing | Machining is where concentricity and surface damage are controlled. |
| Interruption duty | Established by type test on the complete fuse | The tube alone is not type tested; the fuse is. |

Values above describe what is commonly specified for this component class. Confirm the exact figure against the datasheet for the grade being purchased before design release.

## 4. Selection and sizing

- The fuse class and the interruption duty the tube has to survive.
- Whether the design is expulsion or current limiting, which changes the liner requirement.
- The gas generating behaviour required inside the bore during interruption.
- Machined end features and the concentricity tolerance the assembly can accept.
- Whether the tube is a standard size or a replacement for an existing design, in which case the drawing governs.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- IEC 60282-1, high voltage fuses for current limiting fuse designs.
- IEC 60282-2, high voltage fuses for expulsion fuse designs.
- IEEE C37.41, design tests for high voltage fuses and other distribution equipment.
- IEEE C37.42, distribution cutouts and fuse links.
- IEC 60787, application guide for high voltage current limiting fuses.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Handle machined tubes without impact. A machined wall is strongest in compression and weakest against a point load at the thread or hole.
- Keep the bore clean and free of machining dust before the liner is fitted.
- Check concentricity at incoming inspection against the drawing, since it is the tolerance most often lost in transport.
- Store in the original packaging to protect threads and machined ends.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanical burst during interruption | Wall or liner not matched to the interruption duty | Confirm the tube against the fuse design and the type test evidence for that design. |
| Arc ablation of the bore | Liner material or thickness unsuitable for the fault level | Match the liner to the duty, and confirm the type test on the complete fuse. |
| Tube will not assemble into the fuse | Concentricity or bore tolerance outside the drawing | Inspect concentricity and bore at incoming, and hold the drawing tolerance in machining. |
| Thread or hole damage in service | Point loading during assembly or handling | Use the specified assembly tooling and store in protective packaging. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Fuse manufacturers building expulsion and current limiting fuse families.
- Dropout cutout and distribution cutout OEMs.
- Current limiting fuse and switch fuse manufacturers.
- Electrical insulation fabricators converting tubes into fuse assemblies.

